- VSCode Version: 1.41.0
- OS Version: Windows 10, MacOS

Multiple cursors appear in nested snippets after code completion is used several times at tab stop position. This may be caused by miscalculation of tab stop indexes. When each new snippet is added inside the existing one, tab stop indexes of the outer snippet are recalculated so that all tab stops inside the outer snippet and after it are numbered sequentially.
At some point, the index numbers are miscalculated so that multiple tab stops with the same index number appear. When the cursor reaches these tab stops, several cursors appear, as several tab stops have the same index.

### Steps to Reproduce

1. Using VSCode's  'Configure user snippets' command (more info here https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/editor/userdefinedsnippets), create simple snippet definition:
```
"snippet": {
        "scope": "typescript",
        "prefix": "@2",
        "body": [
            "function($1) {$2}"
        ]
}
```
2. Create New Typescript file using New File menu option
3. Start typing @2... Code completion options should appear in a drop down menu. Select @2 option. This will insert function snippet and replace @2 symbols with snippet's text. You should get the following text fragment ( vertical bars | here indicate tab stop positions in the inserted snippet):
```
function( | ) { | } |
```
4. Using Tab button go to the second tab position (which is between curly brackets {} ).
5. Press Enter and then type @2. 
```
function () {
  @2|
}
```
Again code completion drop down menu should appear. Selecting option @2 insert second nested snippet. You should get following text:
```
function() {
    function() {}
}
```
6. Repeat actions from step 5 one more time. Result should be following:
```
function() {
    function() {
        function() { }
   }
}
```
6. Using Tab go to position between curly brackets and ask for code completion there, using arrows select some option, for example undefined, and press Enter. 
```
function() {
    function() {
        function() {undefined}
   }
}
```
7. Press Tab. Cursor should jump to next tab stop( vertical bar | indicates this position )
```
function() {
    function() {
        function() {undefined} |
   }
}
````
8. Press Enter to insert new line. Type @2, insert snippet as described above, press Tab, Ctrl+space, select 'undefined', press Enter, press Tab. Result should be following:
```
function() {
    function() {
        function() {undefined}
        function() {undefined} |
    }
}
```
9. Using Tab for navigation between tab stop positions and repeating steps described above create structure like this. Do NOT use arrows for navigation, only Tab or Shift+Tab!
```
function() {
    function() {
        function() { undefined }
        function() { undefined }
    }
    function() {
        function() { undefined }
        function() { undefined }
        function() { undefined }
        function() { undefined }
        function() { undefined }
        function() {}
    }
}
```
10. After 6-8 code completions next function snippet appears with multiple cursors.

### Expected results

Multiple cursors should not appear.

### Actual results

Multiple cursors appear in the newly inserted snippet.
![vscode-snippets](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/6931493/71067409-24c8f600-217d-11ea-8c6d-ccc338d7a7a5.gif)


This issue occurs even when all extensions are disabled, (Launch with `code --disable-extensions` to check.)

